203:% Secondly, we show the hardness stems from the gradient descent during the training phase. Intuitively, the gradient of the loss function at each iteration can be different, depending on the training data, the loss, initial parameters and the training order. And the difference of the gradient causes the complex interactions between the model and the training data. \warning{Thirdly, we discuss....}
204:% Specifically, we proved the following results: (1) For arbitrary loss function and learning rate, data debugging is NP-hard no matter what training order is considered; (2) If the loss is fixed as the hinge-like function, data debugging can still be NP-hard, as long as the training order is adversarially chosen; (3) Data debugging is solvable in linear time when the loss is extremely simple, such as the linear function. Since most of the machine learning models are trained by gradient descent, the above results suggest that \textcolor{blue}{ proxy models such as KNN may be a right way to design approximate algorithms that enjoy theoretical guarantees.}
210:    \item We studied the computational complexity of data debugging and showed that data debugging is NP-hard for linear classifiers in the general setting for \emph{all possible training orders}.
212:    \item We studied the complexity of \textsc{Debuggable} when the loss is fixed as the hinge-like function. For 2 or higher dimension, \textsc{Debuggable} is NP-complete when the training order is adversarially chosen; For one-dimensional cases, \textsc{Debuggable} can be NP-hard when the interception $\beta<0$, and is solvable in linear time when $\beta \ge 0$.
214:    \item  We proved that \textsc{Debuggable} is solvable in linear time when the loss function is linear.
218:Moreover, we have a discussion on the implications of these complexity results for machine learning interpretability and data quality, as well as limitations of score-based greedy methods. Our results suggest the further study as follows. (1) It is better to characterize the training sample and find the criterion which can be used to decide the existence of efficient algorithms; (2) Designing algorithms with CSP-solver is a potential way to solve data debugging more efficiently than the brute-force one; (3) Developing random algorithms is a potential way to solve data debugging successfully with high probability. 
