   511	\section{Results for Fixed Loss Functions}\label{fixed-loss}
   512	  We have proved the NP-hardness for \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} when the loss function is not fixed. In this section, we study the complexity when the loss function is fixed as linear and hinge-like functions.   
   513	  Assuming that SGD terminates after only one epoch with a fixed order, we will show that \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is solvable in linear time for linear loss. For hinge-like loss functions, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} can be solved in linear time only when the dimension $d=1$ and the interception $\beta\ge 0$. For the rest cases, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} becomes NP-hard. 
   514	
   515	  \subsection{The Easy Case}
   516	    We start with the linear loss function $\mathcal{L}=-\alpha (y\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}+\beta)$, with which all the training data are activated and $\mathbf{w}^* = \mathbf{w}^*(T)=\mathbf{w}^{(0)} + \sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in T}\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x}$.
   517	    Since $y_\text{test}\in \{-1,1\}$, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is equivalent to deciding whether 
   518	    \begin{equation*}
   519	        \max_{T^\prime\subseteq T}\{y_\text{test} (\mathbf{w}^*(T^\prime))^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}\}>0.
   520	    \end{equation*}
   511	\section{Results for Fixed Loss Functions}\label{fixed-loss}
   512	  We have proved the NP-hardness for \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} when the loss function is not fixed. In this section, we study the complexity when the loss function is fixed as linear and hinge-like functions.   
   513	  Assuming that SGD terminates after only one epoch with a fixed order, we will show that \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is solvable in linear time for linear loss. For hinge-like loss functions, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} can be solved in linear time only when the dimension $d=1$ and the interception $\beta\ge 0$. For the rest cases, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} becomes NP-hard. 
   514	
   515	  \subsection{The Easy Case}
   516	    We start with the linear loss function $\mathcal{L}=-\alpha (y\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}+\beta)$, with which all the training data are activated and $\mathbf{w}^* = \mathbf{w}^*(T)=\mathbf{w}^{(0)} + \sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in T}\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x}$.
   517	    Since $y_\text{test}\in \{-1,1\}$, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is equivalent to deciding whether 
   518	    \begin{equation*}
   519	        \max_{T^\prime\subseteq T}\{y_\text{test} (\mathbf{w}^*(T^\prime))^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}\}>0.
   520	    \end{equation*}
   521	     
   522	    A training sample $(\mathbf{x},y)$ is ``good'' if $y_\text{test} (\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x})^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$ and ``bad'' otherwise.
   523	    The \emph{good training-sample assessment} (GTA) algorithm, as shown in Algorithm \ref{algo:gta}, deals with this situation by greedily picking all ``good'' training samples.
   524	    
   525	    % Since all the training samples are independent, 
   526	
   527	
   528	    Denoting $T^*$ as the set of all good data in $T$, it follows that
   529	    \begin{equation*}
   530	        \begin{aligned}
   531	            y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^*(T^*))^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test} &= y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^{(0)})^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test} + \sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in T^*} y_\text{test} (\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x})^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}\\
   532	            &\ge y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^{(0)})^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test} + \sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in T^\prime} y_\text{test} (\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x})^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}
   533	        \end{aligned}
   534	    \end{equation*}
   535	    for all $T^\prime\subseteq T$.
   536	    Hence $\max_{T^\prime\subseteq T}\{y_\text{test} (\mathbf{w}^*(T^\prime))^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}\}=y_\text{test} (\mathbf{w}^*(T^*))^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}$ and \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} can be solved by GTA in linear time. 
   537	    The following theorem is straightforward.
   538	        
   539	  \begin{theorem}\label{thm:linear-loss-linear-time}
   540	      \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is linear time solvable for linear loss functions.
   541	  \end{theorem}
   542	
   543	    % \begin{figure}
   544	    %     \centering
   545	    %     \begin{minipage}{0.4\textwidth}
   546	    %         \begin{tikzpicture}
   547	    %             \draw [->,thick] (0,0)--(1,0.5);
   548	    %             \node [below] at (1,0.2) {$y\mathbf{x}_\text{test}$};
   549	    %             \draw [fill] (0,0) circle [radius=0.05];
   550	    %             \draw [dashed] (-2,4) -- (1,-2);
   551	    %             \draw [->, thick] (0,0)--(-5,0);
   552	    %             \node [below] at (-5,0) {$\mathbf{w}^{(0)}$};
   553	    %             \draw [->, dotted,thick] (-5,0) -- (-4,1) -- (-3,0) -- (-2,2) -- (-1,3) -- (0,1.5) -- (1,1);
   554	    %         \end{tikzpicture}
   555	    %     \end{minipage}
   556	        
   557	    %     \caption{Caption}
   558	    %     \label{fig:enter-label}
   559	    % \end{figure}
   560	    
   561	
   562	
   563	    % \begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.4\textwidth}
   564	    \begin{algorithm}[H]
   565	    \KwIn{Training data $T$, loss function 
   566	    $\mathcal{L}$, initial parameter $\mathbf{w}^{(0)}$, learning rate $\boldsymbol{\eta}$, threshold $\varepsilon$ and test instance $(\mathbf{x}_\text{test},y_\text{test})$.
   567	    }
   568	    \KwOut{TRUE, iff $\texttt{SGD}_\Lambda(\mathcal{L},\boldsymbol{\eta},\varepsilon,T)$ is debuggable on $(\mathbf{x}_\text{test}y_\text{test})$.}
   569	      \SetAlgoLined
   570	      $\mathbf{w}\leftarrow \mathbf{w}^{(0)}$\; 
   571	      \For{$(\mathbf{x},y)\in T$}{
   572	        \If{$y_\text{test} (\alpha y \boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes \mathbf{x})^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$}{
   573	          $\mathbf{w}\leftarrow \mathbf{w}+\alpha y\boldsymbol{\eta}\otimes\mathbf{x}$\;}
   574	      }
   575	      \If{$y_\text{test} \mathbf{w}^\top \mathbf{x}_\text{test} \ge 0$}{return TRUE\;}
   576	      return FALSE\;
   577	      \caption{Good Training-sample Assessment (GTA)}\label{algo:gta}
   578	    \end{algorithm}
   579	    % \end{wrapfigure}
   580	
   581	
   582	    GTA is still effective for one-dimensional classifiers trained with hinge-like losses when $\beta\ge 0$. 
   583	    \begin{theorem}\label{thm:hinge-loss-linear-time}
   584	        \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is linear time solvable for hinge-like loss functions, when $d=1$ and $\beta \ge 0$.
   585	    \end{theorem}
   586	    \begin{proof} 
   587	        It suffices to prove that if $\exists T^\prime\subseteq T$ such that $\texttt{SGD}_\Lambda(T^\prime,x_\text{test})=y_\text{test} $, $\texttt{SGD}_\Lambda(T^*,x_\text{test})=y_\text{test}$.
   588	
   589	        a) Suppose all the data in $T^*$ are activated, we have
   590	        \begin{equation*}
   591	            \begin{aligned}
   592	                y_\text{test} w^*(T^*) x_\text{test} &= y_\text{test} w^{(0)} x_\text{test} + \sum_{(x,y)\in T^*}y_\text{test} \alpha y \eta x x_\text{test} \\
   593	                &\ge y_\text{test} w^{(0)} x_\text{test} + \sum_{(x,y)\in T^\prime\cap T^*}y_\text{test} \alpha y \eta x x_\text{test} +\sum_{(x,y)\in T^\prime\setminus T^*}y_\text{test} \alpha y \eta x x_\text{test} \\
   594	                &= y_\text{test} w^*(T^\prime) x_\text{test} \ge 0
   595	            \end{aligned}
   596	        \end{equation*}
   597	
   598	        b) Suppose $(x,y)\in T^*$ is the first inactivated data during the training phase, and $w$ is the current parameter, we have $ywx>\beta$. Since $\alpha\eta \cdot (xy) \cdot (x_\text{test}y_\text{test}) \ge 0$, we have $(x_\text{test}y_\text{test})\cdot w\ge 0$.
   599	        Let $T^{\prime\prime}$ be the set of training data appeared before $(x,y)$, we have $y_\text{test} w^*(T^*) x_\text{test} \ge y_\text{test} w^*(T^{\prime\prime}) x_\text{test}\ge 0$.
   600	    \end{proof}
   601	
   602	  \subsection{The Hard Case}
   603	
   604	  % However, when $d\ge 2$, the problem becomes NP-hard if the training order is arbitrarily chosen.
   605	  The gradient of training data may not always be activated and could be affected by the training order. 
   606	   When the training order is adversarially chosen, the following theorem shows that 
   607	    \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is NP-hard for all $d\ge 2$ and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}$.
   608	
   609	  \begin{theorem}\label{thm:hinge-hard}
   610	        If the training order is adversarially chosen and $d \ge 2$, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is NP-hard for \emph{each} hinge-like loss function at \emph{every} constant learning rate.
   611	  \end{theorem}
   612	  
   613	  \begin{proof}[Proof sketch.]
   614	   Since the result can be easily extended for all $d>2$ by padding the other $d-2$ dimensions with zeros, we only prove for the case of $d=2$.
   615	    We assume $\beta \ge -1$ and leave the $\beta< -1$ case to the appendix. To avoid cluttering, we further assume $\boldsymbol{\eta}=\mathbf{1}$ and $\alpha=1$. The proof can be easily generalized by appropriately re-scaling the constructed vectors.
   616	    
   617	    We build a reduction from the subset sum problem, which is well-known to be NP-hard:
   618	    \begin{center}
   619	    \begin{minipage}{0.8\textwidth}
   620	    \begin{mdframed}[
   621	        linewidth=1pt,
   622	        roundcorner=5pt
   623	    ]
   624	    \textsc{Subset Sum}
   625	    
   626	    \textbf{Input:} A set of positive integer $S$, and a positive integer $t$.
   627	
   628	    \textbf{Output:} ``Yes'': if $\exists S^\prime\subseteq S$ such that $\sum_{a\in S^\prime}a = t$;\\
   629	    \hspace*{3.7em}``No'': otherwise.
   630	    \end{mdframed}
   631	    \end{minipage}
   632	    \end{center}
   633	
   634	    Suppose $n=|S|$, $m=\max_{a\in S}\{a\}$, $\gamma = \max\{\beta,1\}$ and $S=\{a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n\}$. We further assume $n>1$.
   635	    Let the training data be
   636	    \begin{equation*}
   637	        T=\{(\mathbf{x}_1,y_1),(\mathbf{x}_2,y_2),\dots,(\mathbf{x}_n,y_n)\}\cup\{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b),(\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)\}
   638	    \end{equation*}
   639	    where $\mathbf{x}_iy_i=(\frac{\sqrt{\gamma}}{n+1},3\sqrt{\gamma} a_i) $ for all $1\le i\le n, \mathbf{x}_cy_c=((18n^2m^2-2)\sqrt{\gamma},-3t\sqrt{\gamma}),\mathbf{x}_by_b=(\sqrt{\gamma},-\sqrt{\gamma}), \mathbf{x}_ay_a=(\sqrt{\gamma},\sqrt{\gamma})$. Let $\mathbf{w}^{(0)}=(-18n^2m^2\sqrt{\gamma},0)$.
   640	    Let the test instance $(\mathbf{x}_\text{test},y_\text{test})$ satisfy $\mathbf{x}_\text{test}y_\text{test}=(1,0)$. 
   641	    
   642	    Let the training order be $(\mathbf{x}_1,y_1),(\mathbf{x}_2,y_2),\dots,(\mathbf{x}_n,y_n),(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b),(\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)$.
   643	
   644	    For each $1\le i<n$, suppose $\mathbf{w}^{(0)} \xrightarrow{T\cap \{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)|1\le j\le i\}}\mathbf{w}^{(i)}$, we have
   645	      \begin{equation*}
   646	          \begin{aligned}
   647	              y_{i+1}(\mathbf{w}^{(i)})^\top\mathbf{x}_{i+1} &\le \frac{\sqrt{\gamma}}{n+1}(-18n^2m^2\sqrt{\gamma}+\frac{\sqrt{\gamma}i}{n+1}) + 3\sqrt{\gamma}a_{i+1}\sum_{j=1}^i 3\sqrt{\gamma}a_j\\
   648	              % &\le \gamma\left(\frac{1}{n+1}(-18n^2m^2+\frac{n}{n+1})+9nm^2\right)\\
   649	              &\le \gamma\left( -\frac{n-1}{n+1}\cdot 9nm^2+\frac{n}{(n+1)^2}\right) < -1 \le \beta
   650	          \end{aligned}
   651	      \end{equation*}
   652	      
   653	      This means all the $T\setminus \{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b), (\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)\}$ can be activated. Thus the resulting parameter trained by $T\setminus \{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b), (\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)\}$ is
   654	      \begin{equation*}
   655	          \mathbf{w}_c = \mathbf{w}^{(0)} + \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{x}_iy_i
   656	          =\left( -18n^2m^2\sqrt{\gamma}+\frac{\sqrt{\gamma}|T^*|}{n+1} , 3\sqrt{\gamma} \sum_{i=1}^n a_i\right).
   657	      \end{equation*}
   658	
   659	    It now suffices to prove that for all $S^\prime\subseteq S$, $\sum_{a\in S^\prime} a = t$ if and only if $\exists T^\prime\subseteq T$ such that $\mathbf{w} : \mathbf{w}^{(0)} \xrightarrow{T^\prime}\mathbf{w}$ satisfies $y_\text{test}\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$.
   660	
   661	    \underline{If:} Suppose $\exists S^\prime\subseteq S$ such that $\sum_{a\in S}a=t$, we prove that $\exists T^\prime\subseteq T$ such that $y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^*)^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$ for $\mathbf{w}^*$ satisfying $\mathbf{w}^{(0)}\xrightarrow{T^\prime}\mathbf{w}^*$.
   662	    
   663	    Let $T^*=\{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)|a_i\in S^\prime\}$, $T^\prime = T^*\cup 
   664	    \{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b), (\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)\}$. We have
   665	    \begin{equation*}
   666	        \mathbf{w}_c = (-18n^2m^2\sqrt{\gamma} + \frac{\sqrt{\gamma}|T^*|}{n+1},3\sqrt{\gamma}\sum_{a_i\in S^\prime}a_i)= (-18n^2m^2\sqrt{\gamma} + \frac{\sqrt{\gamma}|T^*|}{n+1},3\sqrt{\gamma}t).
   667	    \end{equation*}
   668	    And therefore $y_c\mathbf{w}_c^\top\mathbf{x}_c=\gamma\left((-18n^2m^2+\frac{|T^*|}{n+1})(18n^2m^2-2) -9t^2 \right)<-1\le \beta$, so
   669	    \begin{equation*}
   670	        \mathbf{w}_c \xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c)}\mathbf{w}_b = \mathbf{w}_c + \mathbf{x}_cy_c = (\sqrt{\gamma}(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2),0).
   671	    \end{equation*}
   672	    Note that $y_b\mathbf{w}_b^\top\mathbf{x}_b=\gamma(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2)< -1 \le \beta$, we have
   673	    \begin{equation*}
   674	        \mathbf{w}_b \xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b)}\mathbf{w}_a = \mathbf{w}_b + \mathbf{x}_ay_a = (\sqrt{\gamma}(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-1),-\sqrt{\gamma})
   675	    \end{equation*}
   676	    Note also that $y_a\mathbf{w}_a^\top\mathbf{x}_a=\gamma(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2)<-1\le \beta$, we have
   677	    \begin{equation*}
   678	        \mathbf{w}_a \xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)}\mathbf{w}^* = \mathbf{w}_a + \mathbf{x}_ay_a = (\frac{|T^*|\sqrt{\gamma}}{n+1},0)
   679	    \end{equation*}
   680	    Therefore, $y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^*)^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}=\frac{|T^*|\sqrt{\gamma}}{n+1}>0$.
   681	
   682	    \underline{Only if:}
   683	    For each $T^\prime\subseteq T$, let $T^*=T'\setminus \{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b), (\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)\}$.
   684	    If $y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^*)^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$ for $\mathbf{w}^*$ satisfying $\mathbf{w}^{(0)}\xrightarrow{T^\prime}\mathbf{w}^*$, we prove that $\exists S^\prime\subseteq S$ such that $\sum_{a\in S^\prime}a=t$. 
   685	    We first show that for each $T^\prime\subseteq T$, if $\mathbf{w}(\mathbf{w}^{(0)}\xrightarrow{T^\prime}\mathbf{w})$ satisfying $y_\text{test}\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}>0$, we have $\forall k\in\{a,b,c\},(\mathbf{x}_k,y_k)\in T^\prime,\warning{y_k\mathbf{w}_k^\top\mathbf{x}_k}<\gamma$, where $\mathbf{w}^{(0)}\xrightarrow{T^*}\mathbf{w}_c\xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c)}\mathbf{w}_b\xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b)}\mathbf{w}_a$. Otherwise, suppose $\exists k \in \{a,b,c\}$ such that $(\mathbf{x}_k,y_k)\not\in T^\prime$ or $y_k\mathbf{w}_k^\top\mathbf{x}_k\ge\gamma$, we have
   686	    \begin{equation*}
   687	    \begin{aligned}
   688	        y_\text{test}\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test} \le 
   689	        % \sqrt{\gamma}\left(-18n^2m^2+\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}+18n^2m^2 -2+1+1-\min\{18n^2m^2,1\}\right)= 
   690	        \sqrt{\gamma}(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-1)<0
   691	    \end{aligned}
   692	    \end{equation*}
   693	    which contradicts to the fact that $y_\text{test}\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}\ge 0$.
   694	
   695	    Let $S^\prime=\{a_i|(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)\in T^*\}$ and $t^\prime=\sum_{a\in S^\prime}a_i$, it suffices to prove $t^\prime=t$.
   696	    Notice that 
   697	    \begin{equation*}
   698	    \begin{aligned}
   699	        \mathbf{w}^{(0)}\xrightarrow{T^*\cap \{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)|1\le j\le i\}}\mathbf{w}_c &= (\sqrt{\gamma}(-18n^2m^2+\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}),3\sqrt{\gamma}\sum_{a_i\in S^\prime}a_i)\\
   700	        &=(\sqrt{\gamma}(-18n^2m^2+\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}),3\sqrt{\gamma}t^\prime)
   701	    \end{aligned}
   702	    \end{equation*}
   703	    Hence $y_c\mathbf{w}_c^\top\mathbf{x}_c=\gamma(-18n^2m^2+\frac{|T^*|}{n+1})(18n^2m^2-2)-9\gamma tt^\prime<-1\le\beta$, thus
   704	    \begin{equation*}
   705	        \mathbf{w}_c\xrightarrow{(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c)}\mathbf{w}_b=\mathbf{w}_c + \mathbf{x}_cy_c = (\sqrt{\gamma}(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2),3\sqrt{\gamma}(t^\prime-t))
   706	    \end{equation*}
   707	
   708	    (1) If $t^\prime\le t-1$, we have $y_b\mathbf{w}_b^\top\mathbf{x}_b=\gamma \left(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2+3(t-t^\prime)\right)> \gamma\ge \beta$, a contradiction.
   709	
   710	    (2) If $t^\prime\ge t+1$, we have $y_a\mathbf{w}_a^\top\mathbf{x}_a=\gamma \left(\frac{|T^*|}{n+1}-2+3(t^\prime-t)\right)> \gamma\ge\beta$, another contradiction.
   711	
   712	    Therefore $t^\prime = t$, and this completes the proof.
   713	  \end{proof}
   714	
   715	    Moreover, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is NP-hard even when $d=1$ and $\beta<0$.
   716	   \begin{theorem}\label{1d-hinge-hard}
   717	       If the training order is adversarially chosen and $d = 1$, \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} remains NP-hard for \emph{each} hinge-like loss function with $\beta<0$ at \emph{every} constant learning rate.
   718	   \end{theorem}
   719	  
   720	 \textbf{Remarks.} The training order in this section can be arbitrary as long as the last three training samples are $(\mathbf{x}_c,y_c),(\mathbf{x}_b,y_b),(\mathbf{x}_a,y_a)$, respectively. All the training samples are ``good'' since for each $(\mathbf{x},y)\in T$ we have $\mathbf{x}^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}yy_\text{test}>0$. This implies that \textsc{Debuggable-Lin} is NP-hard even if all the training data are ``good'' training samples, and exemplifies why the GTA algorithm fails for higher dimensions.
   721	    
   722	\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec-discussion}
   723	% insertion->overfitting
   724	
   725	% \textit{Why we focus only on removing flawed training samples?}
   726	% data augmentation does not work;
   727	% cost of resampling
   728	% cite xxx
   729	% data quality xxx
   730	% cover ce zhang's/xxx's work .
   731	% non misleading
   732	In this paper, we provided a comprehensive analysis on the complexity of \textsc{Debuggable}.
   733	We focus on the linear classifier that is trained using SGD, as it is a key component in the majority of popular models.
   734	
   735	Since \textsc{Debuggable} is a special case of data debugging, the above results proved the intractability of data debugging and therefore gives a negative answer to Problem \ref{open-problem} declared in the introduction. The complexity results also demonstrated that it is not accurate to estimate the impact of subset of training data by summing up the score of each training samples in the subset, \emph{as long as the scores can be calculated in polynomial time}.
   736	
   737	In Section \ref{fixed-loss}, a training sample is said to be ``good'' if it can help the resulting model to predict correctly on the test instance. That is, it can increase $y_\text{test}(\mathbf{w}^*)^\top\mathbf{x}_\text{test}$. 
   738	However, in our proof we showed that \textsc{Debuggable} remains NP-hard even if all training samples are ``good''. This suggests that the quality of a training sample does not depend only on some properties of itself but also on the interaction between the rest of the training data, which should be taken into consideration when developing data cleaning approaches.
   739	
   740	Moreover, the NP-hardness of \textsc{Debuggable}  implies that, it is in general intractable to figure out the causality between even the prediction of a linear classifier and its training data. This may be seem surprising since linear classifiers have long been considered ``inherently interpretable''. As warned in  \cite{Jacovi2020TowardsFI}, \textit{a method being ``inherently interpretable'' needs to be verified before it can be trusted}, the concept of interpretability must be \textit{rigorously defined}, or at least its boundaries specified.
   741	
   742	Our results suggests the following directions for future research. Firstly, characterizing the training sample may be helpful in designing efficient algorithms for data debugging; Secondly, designing algorithms using CSP-solver is a potential way to solve data debugging more efficiently than the brute-force algorithms; Finally, developing random algorithms is a potential way to solve data debugging successfully with high probability.
   743	
   744	% \textbf{Why focus on deleting training samples?}
   745	% Deletion is widely adopted in real-world scenarios where high-quality data is limited and finding an effective data augmentation method is challenging. 

